in 1995 disney made one of the most  amazing films which truly captures the  emotion found between a single father  and his teenage son and it blew  audiences away but then it disappeared a  goofy movie is probably one of the most  underrated films to come out during the  disney renaissance era but it seems to  have completely vanished from pop  culture mentions aside from an  occasional power line t-shirt but why  did this happen and where did it go also  in this video we're going to discuss how  disney actually intended for this movie  to feel nostalgic in the future when it  was being made  [Music]  in this video we're going to talk about  four things emotion the disney formula  nostalgia and disappearance the first  thing we need to discuss though is why a  goofy movie is such a great film  emotion the movie starts us off with our  main character max goof in a pretty  emotional nightmare  this scene depicts max's main internal  struggle throughout the film that he's  terrified about turning into his dad  goofy it's at this point in the movie  where we realize that the fun-loving and  always kind character goofy is actually  the movie's villain if max is our main  character even though he may make tons  of mistakes along the way then goofy is  the villain because he's the one person  who hinders max from achieving what he  wants in the film which is being cool  and getting the girl max's interactions  in the first few scenes with his friends  and his love interest roxanne perfectly  encapsulate the awkwardness of high  school and new relationships  [Laughter]  also it's so much fun to see carl from  jimmy neutron and paulie shore from  encino man join the cast of voice actors  for the film as max's high school  friends as well as the princess brides  wallace shawn as the school principal  inconceivable  there are tons of scenes which are able  to perfectly show the emotion between a  loving and goofy father and an angsty  teenage son goofy takes the  stereotypical pun making dad to the next  level pushing max's embarrassment to the  extreme by taking him on a cross-country  fishing trip complete with a stop at a  possum themed amusement park  honestly though goofy makes such a great  single dad it's simply a complete lack  of communication that causes the rift  between them leading to what i'd call  anti-chemistry during the song on the  open road the two actually sing in canon  perfectly imitating each other's  melodies but just not at the same time  [Music]  although the two don't exactly see eye  to eye get it they're still able to have  great musical chemistry together you can  definitely tell that there just needs to  be an adjustment in their communication  style or some form of compromise for the  two to exit the realm of anti-chemistry  and enter the realm of true chemistry at  one point in the movie after a huge  fallout the two have this interaction  i've grown up  i've got my own life now i know that  i just wanted to be part of it this  devastating interaction resulted from a  total betrayal of trust on max's part  after being officially named the trip's  navigator by his dad max changed the map  directions and sent them towards los  angeles rather than their fishing  destination so he could see his favorite  musician power line goofy hoping he  wouldn't do this let max keep his  navigator status which ultimately led to  max's betrayal after this betrayal there  are no musical numbers for a good  portion of the movie for an enhanced  emotional effect which brings us to the  next item to discuss the disney formula  disney movies tend to follow a pretty  strict formula for success they're  almost always musicals or include  musical numbers they typically feature a  more traditional family unit they  include what's called an i want song as  well as an intro song and there's almost  always a romantic story at the center of  the plot basically the formula is that a  princess with a desire to do more finds  a guy that fulfills her desire along  with plenty of musical numbers to fill  in some time this movie however diverts  almost completely from this formula  taking its own unique one while the film  does include musical numbers throughout  it takes a different angle on how to  fill out the plot instead of having an  intro song such as mulan's honor to us  all in addition to an i want song such  as ariel's part of your world max sings  after today which accomplishes the  functions of both an intro song and an i  want song  it introduces max as a high school  reject planning something big for the  last day of school before summer begins  and it also shows his deepest desires to  finally be seen as cool and to talk to  roxanne another way the film detracts  from the disney formula is by giving us  a less traditional family unit a single  father and son it's really refreshing to  see a less traditional family on screen  when we're used to seeing the typical  nuclear family in most releases showing  how a single dad is able to reach his  son on a deeper emotional level could  actually be quite inspiring to people  going through similar situations  lastly while there is a romantic story  in the film between max and roxanne it  isn't the central focus instead the  focus is placed upon the relationship  between max and goofy so rather than  being a romantic love story like we see  in most disney movies this film is a  family love story in fact i'd argue that  the song nobody else but you is one of  the most touching love songs we got from  the disney renaissance era  the two most memorable songs from this  movie are definitely eye to eye and  stand out stand out is the song that  every kid wishes they had the guts to  perform on the last day of school so  they can finally be seen as cool and get  the girl so i think it's one of the most  universally relatable songs from the  entire movie eye to eye on the other  hand is the culmination of the plot  resolving the two main conflicts of the  story max and goofy finally lose their  anti-chemistry by seeing eye to eye and  max gets to perform on stage with  powerline to impress roxanne re-watching  this movie you can just tell that  there's something about these songs that  pulls you back into the 1990s in a way  none of the other disney songs from this  era can do you can't help but start to  feel sentimental when listening to these  songs but i actually think disney  intended to make us feel this way when  they were making the movie which brings  us to the next topic nostalgia  rewatching this movie i couldn't help  but think that the creators intended to  make a nostalgia time bomb in a goofy  movie specifically for millennials the  score for the film and the genres chosen  for the musical numbers helped to set  the tone of a 1990s high school movie  and there are tons of little things that  point towards its nostalgic feel like  their phones or manual car windows  at the beginning of the movie max is  seen as somewhat of a marty mcfly type  not really that cool but still talented  and pretty likable after his standout  performance though he basically turns  into the super popular ferris bueller he  becomes the main topic of conversation  in the school and there's even a  sequence where he runs in people's  backyards and through a family's house  just the same as ferris bueller did at  the end of his day off because of these  inclusions and many more you can tell  that the creators of the film truly  wanted kids to put themselves in max's  shoes and upon re-watching the movie  later in life recognize how fondly they  remember those times  life moves pretty fast  you don't stop and look around once in a  while  you could miss it ultimately though  because the movie was seemingly made to  create a feeling of nostalgia it fell  out of popularity pretty quickly as  trends changed which leads us to our  last topic disappearance  a goofy movie seemed to slowly disappear  from pop culture in the years fo